✅ Step-by-Step Guide to Push Your Project to GitHub
1. Navigate to Your Project Directory

Open your terminal or command prompt and change to your project directory:

cd path/to/your/project


Replace path/to/your/project with the actual path to your project folder.

2. Initialize a Git Repository (If Not Already Done)

If your project isn't already a Git repository, initialize it:

git init

3. Add Your Files to the Staging Area

Stage all the files in your project for commit:

git add .


This command stages all changes in your project directory.

4. Commit Your Changes

Commit the staged files with a meaningful message:

git commit -m "Initial commit"

5. Add the Remote Repository

Link your local repository to your GitHub repository:

git remote add origin https://github.com/Yash19k/FSWD-1.git

6. Push Your Changes to GitHub

Push your local commits to the main branch of your GitHub repository:

git push -u origin main


If your default branch is named master, replace main with master.

⚠️ Handling Potential Errors

If you encounter the error:

! [rejected] main -> main (fetch first)
error: failed to push some refs to 'https://github.com/Yash19k/FSWD-1.git'
hint: Updates were rejected because the remote contains work that you do not
hint: have locally. This is usually caused by another repository pushing to
hint: the same ref. If you want to integrate the remote changes, use
hint: 'git pull' before pushing again.


This indicates that the remote repository has commits that your local repository doesn't have. To resolve this:

Pull the Latest Changes from GitHub:

git pull origin main --allow-unrelated-histories


This command fetches and merges the changes from the remote repository into your local repository.

Resolve Any Merge Conflicts:

If there are merge conflicts, Git will notify you. Open the conflicting files, resolve the conflicts, and then stage the resolved files:

git add .


Commit the Merge:

After resolving conflicts, commit the merge:

git commit -m "Resolved merge conflicts"


Push the Changes to GitHub:

Finally, push your changes:

git push origin main

🔄 Future Workflow

To keep your local and remote repositories synchronized:

Pull the Latest Changes Before Starting Work:

git pull origin main


Make Your Changes Locally.

Stage and Commit Your Changes:

git add .
git commit -m "Describe your changes"


Push Your Changes to GitHub:

git push origin main